# PHOLD offsite GPU bundle

This folder contains everything needed to run the **`phold proteins-predict`** step of the metagear `structures` workflow on a GPU server, without Nextflow. After running, copy the outputs back to the source machine and re-invoke `metagear structures` to finish the workflow on CPU.

The split is deliberate: only ProstT5 inference (PHOLD_PREDICT) is GPU-heavy. The downstream Foldseek search (PHOLD_COMPARE) is fast on CPU and stays in the Nextflow pipeline.

---

## What's in this folder

| File / dir | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `README.md` | This document |
| `shards/` | The protein FASTA shards to predict on (`__NUM_SHARDS__` files) |
| `viral_join_table.tsv` | Auxiliary table consumed by the *resume* phase on the source machine. Carry-through only; not used here. |
| `run_phold_predict.sh` | The script you run on the GPU server (see below) |
| `manifest.json` | List of expected outputs — used by the source machine to validate completeness on resume |

**Total size:** approximately `__BUNDLE_SIZE_MB__` MB.

---

## Step 1 — Get this bundle onto the GPU server

```bash
rsync -avzP <source>/structures/offsite_predict/  <user>@<gpu-host>:/path/on/gpu-host/
```

If the GPU server shares a filesystem with the source machine (e.g. a DSS mount visible on both), no copy is needed — just `cd` into the folder on the GPU side.

---

## Step 2 — Set up the runtime (one-time)

The GPU server needs:

- **A CUDA-capable GPU** (any Nvidia card with ≥ 8 GB VRAM works; an H100/A100 finishes in a fraction of the time)
- **The `phold` binary on PATH**, with PyTorch + CUDA support
- **A complete PHOLD database installed locally** (`phold install -d <path>`, ~13 GB)

### Easiest path: micromamba env (user-space, no admin needed)

```bash
# One-time install of micromamba in $HOME
curl -Ls https://micro.mamba.pm/api/micromamba/linux-64/latest | tar -xvj bin/micromamba
./bin/micromamba shell init --shell bash --root-prefix=$HOME/micromamba
source ~/.bashrc

# Create the env (~10 min, ~10 GB)
micromamba create -n metagear-structures \
    -c conda-forge -c bioconda \
    phold=__PHOLD_VERSION__ \
    -y

# Verify
micromamba activate metagear-structures
phold --version    # expect __PHOLD_VERSION__
python3 -c "import torch; print('CUDA:', torch.cuda.is_available(), torch.cuda.get_device_name(0))"
```

### Install the PHOLD DB (one-time)

If you haven't already:

```bash
micromamba activate metagear-structures
phold install -d /path/to/your/phold_db -t 8
```

This downloads the ProstT5 weights (~11 GB) and the Foldseek structural index (~2 GB). ~30 minutes on a normal link. Mark the directory path — you'll pass it to `run_phold_predict.sh`.

If a DB is already in place from a previous metagear install, just point at it.

### Alternative paths

- **NVIDIA Pyxis / Enroot:** point the bundled script at the phold container directly; the script doesn't care how phold got on PATH, only that it's there.
- **Singularity:** `singularity exec --nv ... run_phold_predict.sh ...` works the same.

---

## Step 3 — Get a GPU allocation (if you're on a SLURM cluster)

Example for the LRZ-HGX H100 partition:

```bash
salloc -p lrz-hgx-h100-94x4 --gres=gpu:1 -c 6 --mem=60G --time=__SUGGESTED_WALLTIME__
export JOBID=$SLURM_JOB_ID
srun --jobid $JOBID --pty bash
```

On a personal workstation with a GPU, skip this step.

---

## Step 4 — Run

```bash
micromamba activate metagear-structures   # if you went that route
cd /path/to/offsite_predict

bash run_phold_predict.sh --phold-db /path/to/your/phold_db
```

Useful flags:

```bash
# Bigger batch on big GPUs (H100/A100 with >= 16 GB free VRAM)
bash run_phold_predict.sh --phold-db /path/to/phold_db --batch-size 16

# Drop to batch_size=1 on small consumer GPUs (e.g. RTX 3070 8 GB)
bash run_phold_predict.sh --phold-db /path/to/phold_db --batch-size 1

# Continue an interrupted run (skip shards already completed)
bash run_phold_predict.sh --phold-db /path/to/phold_db --resume

# Preview without executing
bash run_phold_predict.sh --phold-db /path/to/phold_db --dry-run
```

### Expected runtime

`__NUM_SHARDS__` shards × `__SHARD_SIZE__` proteins each:

| Hardware | Approx. per-shard | Total |
|---|---|---|
| H100 80GB (batch=16) | 1–3 min | `__ETA_H100__` |
| A100 40GB (batch=8) | 3–6 min | `__ETA_A100__` |
| V100 16GB (batch=8) | 4–8 min | `__ETA_V100__` |
| T4 16GB / RTX 3070 8GB (batch=1) | 8–20 min | `__ETA_T4__` |
| CPU fallback (8 threads, batch=1) | 60–120 min | `__ETA_CPU__` |

---

## Step 5 — Verify

```bash
# Should equal __NUM_SHARDS__
ls outputs/predict_*/phold_aa.fasta | wc -l
```

Spot-check one output dir for sanity:

```bash
ls outputs/predict_input_subset.part_001/
# Expect:
#   phold_aa.fasta
#   phold_3di.fasta
#   phold_prostT5_3di_mean_probabilities.csv
#   phold_prostT5_3di_all_probabilities.json
```

---

## Step 6 — Ship results back

```bash
rsync -avzP outputs/  <user>@<source-host>:<outdir>/structures/phold_predict/
```

`<outdir>` is the same `--outdir` you used in the original `metagear structures` call.

---

## Step 7 — Resume on the source machine

Re-invoke metagear with **the same arguments minus `--structures_prepare_for_gpu`**:

```bash
metagear structures \
    --input  inputs/samplesheet.csv \
    --outdir <same-outdir> \
    --reuse-outputs \
    --structures_scope <same-scope-as-before>
```

`--reuse-outputs` auto-discovers the rsync'd predict outputs at `<outdir>/structures/phold_predict/` and injects them via `--phold_predict_dir`. The pipeline resumes from `MERGE_PHOLD_PREDICTIONS` and runs `PHOLD_COMPARE` + the viral join on CPU. Final annotations land at `<outdir>/annotations/phold/`.

If any shards are missing, the pipeline exits with a clear "shards X, Y, Z missing — re-run them" message before doing any CPU work.

---

## Troubleshooting

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| `phold: command not found` | Env not activated | `micromamba activate <env_name>` first |
| `CUDA not available` inside python | Missing `--nv` (Singularity) or wrong container | For Singularity add `--nv`; for Pyxis check `--container-image=` |
| `RuntimeError: CUDA out of memory` | `batch_size` too high for VRAM | Drop to `--batch-size 1` or `--batch-size 4` |
| `No CA certificates were loaded from the system` (from hf-xet) | Container without CA roots | Already handled — script exports `HF_HUB_DISABLE_XET=1` |
| Shard hangs at 0% for >5 min | First-run ProstT5 warm-up (downloads tokenizer cache) | Wait — usually unstuck after ~3 min on first shard, then fast |
| Some shards finished, some failed | Mixed exit codes | Use `--resume` to retry only the failed ones |

If you hit something not on this list, save `outputs/predict_<shard>/phold_proteins-predict_*.log` from the failing shard and report back — the log captures everything PHOLD did.
